      "t": "Я её законный муж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "По контракту мы родственников пускаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Откройте мне дверь, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, Гончарова уже родила?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, has Goncharova given birth yet?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'hello' and finishing on 'gave birth'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Ещё нет, ждите здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not yet, wait here.' The grammar packs 'else' and 'here' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Чего она трубку не берёт?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why is she not picking up the phone?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'what' and finishing on 'takes'. the doctor answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "Мужчина, как вы себе роды представляете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sir, how do you imagine childbirth?' In Russian, the idea of 'man' flows naturally into 'to imagine' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, привет, всё нормально?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, hi, is everything alright?' In Russian, the idea of 'yes' flows naturally into 'normally' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я уже в роддоме.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am already at the maternity hospital.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'maternity hospital' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Повиси секундочку на линии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hold on the line for a second.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'hold on' before landing on 'line'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скажите, а как мне пройти?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tell me, how can I get through?' The grammar packs 'say' and 'take place' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "А вы вообще кто?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who are you anyway?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'and' and finishing on 'who'. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я её законный муж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am her lawful husband.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'husband' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "doctor",
      "t": "По контракту мы родственников пускаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Under the contract we let close relatives in.' The meaning hinges on 'according to' working together with 'to let in' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Откройте мне дверь, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Open the door for me, please.' In Russian, the idea of 'open' flows naturally into 'please' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
